74
00:03:05,480 --> 00:03:08,285
In a circular buffer, you've got

75
00:03:08,285 --> 00:03:11,480
a set of values that
you're storing and you

76
00:03:11,480 --> 00:03:13,850
have some input and
output pointer that

77
00:03:13,850 --> 00:03:17,370
can say where you're
at in the buffer.

78
00:03:17,370 --> 00:03:19,640
The initial state would
be something like this,

79
00:03:19,640 --> 00:03:21,830
where both the input and output
pointers are pointing to

80
00:03:21,830 --> 00:03:23,360
the same value and that

81
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:26,130
means that in this case
that you're empty.

82
00:03:26,270 --> 00:03:32,780
If you write a new
value in this case,

83
00:03:32,780 --> 00:03:35,030
then you're going to
essentially implement

84
00:03:35,030 --> 00:03:37,340
the input pointer and write to

85
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:39,470
the output pointer
the command that

86
00:03:39,470 --> 00:03:42,220
you wrote inside that
circular buffer.

87
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:45,600
The way we're defining
it for Assignment 7

88
00:03:45,600 --> 00:03:48,725
and later for Assignment 8
is we're going to be writing

89
00:03:48,725 --> 00:03:55,410
entries into our circular
buffer that are a pointer

90
00:03:55,410 --> 00:04:02,345
to some const char
so some string,

91
00:04:02,345 --> 00:04:04,340
essentially that we're going to

92
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:06,900
store inside the entry and

93
00:04:06,900 --> 00:04:08,240
then a size value that tells us

94
00:04:08,240 --> 00:04:11,975
how many bytes are
associated with that buffer.

95
00:04:11,975 --> 00:04:15,890
We're essentially making
these entries based

96
00:04:15,890 --> 00:04:17,735
on the number of

97
00:04:17,735 --> 00:04:20,525
circular buffer entries that
we have available here.

98
00:04:20,525 --> 00:04:22,610
Every time we write to an entry,

99
00:04:22,610 --> 00:04:24,170
we're going to store
these two values,

100
00:04:24,170 --> 00:04:25,685
a pointer to a buffer and a size

101
00:04:25,685 --> 00:04:28,075
in one of the circular
buffer entries.

102
00:04:28,075 --> 00:04:30,620
Then once we stored a command,

103
00:04:30,620 --> 00:04:32,750
we'd increment the input
pointer so that the next time

104
00:04:32,750 --> 00:04:36,000
we wrote we would write
to this new location.

105
00:04:36,110 --> 00:04:38,830
Then if you think
about the case where I

106
00:04:38,830 --> 00:04:41,170
haven't read anything
out yet and I've

107
00:04:41,170 --> 00:04:45,685
written to everything but
one item in my buffer,

108
00:04:45,685 --> 00:04:48,655
where my buffer is just
defined as an array of values,

109
00:04:48,655 --> 00:04:50,530
then it would look
something like this,

110
00:04:50,530 --> 00:04:52,390
where my input
pointer is pointing

111
00:04:52,390 --> 00:04:54,220
at the very last
item in the buffer.

112
00:04:54,220 --> 00:04:56,320
The next time I
wrote a value there,

113
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:59,020
I'd write to the buffer and
then I'd roll back from

114
00:04:59,020 --> 00:05:00,280
the last value to

115
00:05:00,280 --> 00:05:02,110
the first value and the
buffer would be full.

116
00:05:02,110 --> 00:05:04,480
If you remember
our original state

117
00:05:04,480 --> 00:05:07,060
where the input pointer
equals the output pointer,

118
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:09,595
we started with that and
the buffer was empty.

119
00:05:09,595 --> 00:05:11,380
It'll be important
for us to know

120
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:13,015
the difference
between these two,

121
00:05:13,015 --> 00:05:15,460
where if the input pointer

122
00:05:15,460 --> 00:05:17,080
has rolled from the end to back

123
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:18,400
to the beginning we're in

124
00:05:18,400 --> 00:05:21,170
a full buffer state as
opposed to an empty state.

125
00:05:21,170 --> 00:05:22,940
If you implement this way,

126
00:05:22,940 --> 00:05:24,890
you'll need to track the
fact that you rolled

127
00:05:24,890 --> 00:05:28,380
the pointer from the end of
the buffer to the beginning.

128
00:05:28,380 --> 00:05:33,430
Now if you read a value
from the output pointer,

129
00:05:33,430 --> 00:05:36,880
you now have a new
location where you can

130
00:05:36,880 --> 00:05:41,540
store a command in
the circular buffer.

131
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,560
The way you would

132
00:05:47,560 --> 00:05:50,095
define it if you weren't
reading any values,

133
00:05:50,095 --> 00:05:51,850
if you wanted to find
a circular buffer

134
00:05:51,850 --> 00:05:53,530
such that you can keep writing

135
00:05:53,530 --> 00:06:00,535
even before an output
value is read,

136
00:06:00,535 --> 00:06:03,220
then the output pointer
is going to have to

137
00:06:03,220 --> 00:06:06,895
increment when you add
something to the buffer.

138
00:06:06,895 --> 00:06:08,635
The way we've defined it for

139
00:06:08,635 --> 00:06:10,975
assignment 7 and assignment 8,

140
00:06:10,975 --> 00:06:14,350
the value that you read
is always going to be

141
00:06:14,350 --> 00:06:22,450
the last of the size of the
buffer values available.

142
00:06:22,450 --> 00:06:24,580
If we're storing a total of,

143
00:06:24,580 --> 00:06:25,975
let's say 10 values,

144
00:06:25,975 --> 00:06:27,625
and we've written all 10,

145
00:06:27,625 --> 00:06:29,530
and then we want to
write one new value,

146
00:06:29,530 --> 00:06:30,580
you'd have two options.

147
00:06:30,580 --> 00:06:32,350
One is you could block

