150
00:06:35,740 --> 00:06:38,680
but you could also increment
the output pointer so that

151
00:06:38,680 --> 00:06:41,380
the oldest value
gets thrown away

152
00:06:41,380 --> 00:06:42,700
and you still have 10 values

153
00:06:42,700 --> 00:06:44,755
available that are
the 10 most recent.

154
00:06:44,755 --> 00:06:47,170
That second implementation
is what we're going to use

155
00:06:47,170 --> 00:06:49,480
for assignment 7
and assignment 8.

156
00:06:49,480 --> 00:06:51,685
In the way we're defining
the circular buffer,

157
00:06:51,685 --> 00:06:53,860
when you write that
one extra value,

158
00:06:53,860 --> 00:06:55,240
we're going to move
both the input

159
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:57,190
and the output pointer to

160
00:06:57,190 --> 00:06:59,230
the next location and
you're still going to have

161
00:06:59,230 --> 00:07:02,750
the last n number of
values available.

